1
00:00:00,133 --> 00:00:03,250
{fluent}Hello, and a very warm welcome to Shah Spectrum.

2
00:00:03,250 --> 00:00:05,390
If you're new here, welcome.

3
00:00:05,390 --> 00:00:10,249
If you're back again, it's lovely to have you with us once more.

4
00:00:10,976 --> 00:00:16,242
How does a stock market lose four hundred and eighty billion dollars in under four

5
00:00:16,242 --> 00:00:16,842
months?

6
00:00:16,465 --> 00:00:22,316
In China, one group of artificial intelligence and chip shares has done exactly

7
00:00:22,316 --> 00:00:22,916
that.

8
00:00:22,538 --> 00:00:26,543
And it tells us something about how excitement works in markets.

9
00:00:27,264 --> 00:00:28,602
Hi, I'm Mittal Shah.

10
00:00:28,602 --> 00:00:32,426
Stay with me for five minutes, and this will make sense.

11
00:00:33,138 --> 00:00:36,657
The group is tracked by an index called the STAR fifty.

12
00:00:36,657 --> 00:00:38,604
An index is simply a scoreboard:

13
00:00:38,604 --> 00:00:42,882
it follows a basket of shares and tells you how the basket is doing.

14
00:00:42,882 --> 00:00:48,587
The STAR fifty follows fifty technology companies listed on a board of the Shanghai

15
00:00:48,587 --> 00:00:51,511
stock exchange, many of them chip and AI firms.

16
00:00:51,511 --> 00:00:56,812
According to news reports from the twenty-sixth and twenty-eighth of October, it has

17
00:00:56,812 --> 00:00:59,902
fallen about forty per cent in less than four months.

18
00:00:59,902 --> 00:01:05,203
That wiped out roughly four hundred and eighty billion dollars of market value, and

19
00:01:05,203 --> 00:01:08,531
it ended a rally that had run for about two years.

20
00:01:09,216 --> 00:01:11,562
Was it only this corner of the market?

21
00:01:11,562 --> 00:01:12,162
No.

22
00:01:11,775 --> 00:01:16,476
China's wider blue-chip index, the CSI three hundred, is about twenty per cent below

23
00:01:16,476 --> 00:01:17,719
its peak in June.

24
00:01:17,719 --> 00:01:22,937
The Nasdaq in the United States is down about six per cent from a record.

25
00:01:22,937 --> 00:01:26,988
So AI shares have wobbled elsewhere too, but far less.

26
00:01:27,743 --> 00:01:29,731
Why did this one fall so hard?

27
00:01:29,731 --> 00:01:32,216
The reports name no single trigger.

28
00:01:32,216 --> 00:01:33,755
Traders pointed to three worries:

29
00:01:33,755 --> 00:01:38,003
very high prices, tax bills, and the risk that Washington or others bar Chinese

30
00:01:38,003 --> 00:01:39,773
equipment from their data centres.

31
00:01:40,546 --> 00:01:42,105
Start with the prices.

32
00:01:42,105 --> 00:01:46,514
There is a simple measure called the price-to-earnings ratio.

33
00:01:46,514 --> 00:01:51,421
It asks how many times a company's yearly profit the share price is worth.

34
00:01:51,421 --> 00:01:56,997
If a share costs one hundred rupees and the company earns ten rupees per share in a

35
00:01:56,997 --> 00:01:58,197
year, the ratio is ten.

36
00:01:58,197 --> 00:02:04,210
At its peak, the STAR fifty traded at nearly two hundred and fifty times earnings.

37
00:02:04,210 --> 00:02:07,722
Even after the fall, it is around one hundred.

38
00:02:07,722 --> 00:02:13,940
The Nasdaq one hundred, a basket of big American tech companies, is around thirty.

39
00:02:13,940 --> 00:02:17,904
A high ratio is not wrong in itself, but it means a lot of future good news is

40
00:02:17,904 --> 00:02:19,631
already in the price.

41
00:02:20,419 --> 00:02:21,588
Now, who was buying?

42
00:02:21,588 --> 00:02:25,395
Many of the buyers were ordinary households.

43
00:02:25,395 --> 00:02:27,132
Take a robot maker called You-nee-tree.

44
00:02:27,132 --> 00:02:32,809
When it listed in August, ordinary investors applied for its shares about eight

45
00:02:32,809 --> 00:02:34,271
thousand times over.

46
00:02:34,271 --> 00:02:37,389
That is what oversubscribed means:

47
00:02:37,389 --> 00:02:39,404
far more people asked for shares than were on offer.

48
00:02:39,404 --> 00:02:44,361
Its shares have roughly halved since they began trading.

49
00:02:44,361 --> 00:02:49,041
Two chip designers, MetaX and Moore Threads, jumped seven hundred per cent and four

50
00:02:49,041 --> 00:02:52,045
hundred per cent on their first day last year.

51
00:02:52,045 --> 00:02:54,623
Both are now down about half since July.

52
00:02:54,623 --> 00:02:59,907
Two makers of optical parts for data centres, Zhongji Innolight and Eoptolink, are

53
00:02:59,907 --> 00:03:02,513
down about sixty per cent from their summer highs.

54
00:03:03,173 --> 00:03:05,562
There is also a policy angle.

55
00:03:05,562 --> 00:03:10,809
The reports say Beijing has been steering household savings towards shares, and that

56
00:03:10,809 --> 00:03:16,721
regulators have been pushing to curb borrowed money and high-speed computer trading.

57
00:03:16,721 --> 00:03:20,823
The same reporting says state-backed investors were sellers into the rally.

58
00:03:20,823 --> 00:03:25,655
So when prices fall, the pain lands on families, not only on funds.

59
00:03:25,655 --> 00:03:30,820
One investor quoted said he bought because he believed the government stood behind

60
00:03:30,820 --> 00:03:31,465
You-nee-tree.

61
00:03:31,465 --> 00:03:33,294
He is now nursing losses.

62
00:03:34,025 --> 00:03:37,111
And here is why a fall in one corner matters.

63
00:03:37,111 --> 00:03:42,282
According to Goldman Sachs, AI-related companies make up forty-two per cent of the

64
00:03:42,282 --> 00:03:46,815
total value of Chinese shares, and fifty-seven per cent of the trading.

65
00:03:46,815 --> 00:03:53,310
When so much of a market sits in one theme, a fall in that theme is felt across the

66
00:03:53,310 --> 00:03:54,615
whole market.

67
00:03:55,443 --> 00:03:56,538
What do we not know?

68
00:03:56,538 --> 00:03:57,787
Where prices go next.

69
00:03:57,787 --> 00:03:59,192
Nobody does.

70
00:03:59,192 --> 00:04:04,493
The reports say no clear trigger was identified, and the American move against

71
00:04:04,493 --> 00:04:06,224
Chinese suppliers has not been completed.

72
00:04:06,224 --> 00:04:11,254
These figures come from news reports, not from the exchange or the index provider,

73
00:04:11,254 --> 00:04:17,345
and different outlets give slightly different Nasdaq numbers, so treat them as

74
00:04:17,345 --> 00:04:18,467
approximate.

75
00:04:19,099 --> 00:04:19,731
To sum up:

76
00:04:19,731 --> 00:04:24,190
prices can rise much faster than the profits behind them when a story is exciting,

77
00:04:24,190 --> 00:04:26,910
and fall just as fast when the mood turns.

78
00:04:26,910 --> 00:04:32,600
The price-to-earnings ratio is one simple way to see how much excitement is already

79
00:04:32,600 --> 00:04:33,200
built in.

80
00:04:33,744 --> 00:04:37,516
This is news and explanation, not investment advice.

81
00:04:38,222 --> 00:04:43,311
And before you go, we looked at the huge sums being spent on AI computing on the

82
00:04:43,311 --> 00:04:47,155
twenty-sixth of October, in our episode "Big AI spending:

83
00:04:47,155 --> 00:04:48,682
when does it pay back?".

84
00:04:48,682 --> 00:04:50,721
The link is in the description.

85
00:04:50,721 --> 00:04:54,036
Thanks for listening, and see you in the next episode.

